            <ArticleTitle>Emotions in communication skills training - experiences from general practice to Porsche maintenance.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>2141-2143</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0738-3991(17)30350-6</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.pec.2017.06.006</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>The emphasis on skills in communication training of physicians has gained momentum over the last 30 years. Furthermore, a specific focus on skills to address emotions has been suggested and more recently supported by empirical studies. In this paper we use the Expanded Four Habits Model to illustrate how a structured consultation model supplemented with specific skills to address emotions is considered useful in medical and non-medical settings. The primacy of emotions in different types of professional encounters is discussed in relation to education and practice.</AbstractText>
